\section{A Word Falls into Space}

Place a single word in front of a contemporary language model: \emph{mother}.

On the page it is compact with lullabies and court orders, nursery rhymes and immigration law. Inside the machine it becomes something stranger. The four letters are first sliced into one or more tokens from a fixed vocabulary. Each token is then mapped to a point in a high-dimensional space. Thousands of numbers snap into place.

Meaning acquires co-ordinates.

Those co-ordinates are not chosen by lexicographers. They are the end state of a long optimisation: months of sweeping across billions of sentences, asking the same narrow question over and over---given this sequence of tokens, what comes next? Each time the model predicts the wrong continuation, its internal parameters are nudged. The token representing \emph{mother} moves slightly in response to every context where it helped or harmed a prediction. Somewhere in that space, a cloud of points shifts until error rates are minimised.

The result is an embedding space: a geometric field where each token inhabits a location $e(w)$, a vector of, say, 1024 real numbers. Two locations sit close together not because some committee has declared their meanings related, but because, in the archive the model has absorbed, they turn up in similar neighbourhoods.

The old metaphors of ``semantic fields'' and ``spaces of meaning'' harden here into literal machinery. A distance between words becomes a number. A direction becomes an operation. An angle becomes a measure of how often two tokens stand in for each other in practice.

The astonishing fact is not that engineers found a clever way to represent words numerically. It is that, in the process, vast textual histories are being compacted into a geometry that can be traversed, recombined, and governed.

\section{The Geometry That Remembers}

\subsection{Distances as Compressed History}

Consider two tokens, $w_1$ and $w_2$, mapped to vectors $e(w_1)$ and $e(w_2)$ in a $d$-dimensional space. The model normalises these vectors so they all lie roughly on the surface of a hypersphere. The quantity that matters is then the angle between them. Its cosine,
\[
\cos \theta = \frac{e(w_1)\cdot e(w_2)}{\lVert e(w_1)\rVert\;\lVert e(w_2)\rVert},
\]
runs from $-1$ to $1$ and becomes a numerical answer to a question that used to be qualitative: how similar are the contexts in which these tokens appear?

When we inspect these angles, familiar patterns emerge. The point for \emph{doctor} lies close to those for \emph{hospital}, \emph{patient}, \emph{diagnosis}. The point for \emph{algorithm} neighbours \emph{model}, \emph{data}, \emph{training}. Semantic neighbourhoods that human readers have inhabited for centuries materialise again as clumps on the hypersphere.

Yet these distances also expose the archive's wounds. If training texts associate \emph{engineer} far more often with \emph{he} than with \emph{she}, the embedding for \emph{engineer} tilts toward masculine pronouns. If news and policy documents mention certain countries predominantly in connection with crisis or corruption, their points sink into regions saturated with those tones. No explicit ``bias term'' is added later. The inclination is baked into the terrain.

A cosine similarity score is therefore not merely a technical curiosity. It is a compact trace of institutional memory: which topics have shared pages, which roles have been jointly narrated, which populations have been allowed to speak in the same breath. The field does not know history, but it remembers it.

\subsection{Directions as Reusable Relations}

Distances are only half the story. The other half lies in differences. Take two points $e(w_1)$ and $e(w_2)$. Their difference
\[
d = e(w_2) - e(w_1)
\]
is a direction in space. Apply that direction to a third point $e(w_3)$: we obtain $e(w_3) + d$. In general this will not land exactly on an existing token, but in practice the nearest neighbours to this target often share a relational pattern with the original pair.

This phenomenon was first popularised when Mikolov and colleagues showed that the vector arithmetic
\[
e(\text{``king''}) - e(\text{``man''}) + e(\text{``woman''})
\]
lands near $e(\text{``queen''})$ \cite{mikolov2013efficient}. The direction from \emph{man} to \emph{woman} applied to \emph{king} moves one toward \emph{queen}. Similar arithmetic recovers singular from plural, present from past, country from capital.

The important point is not the party trick. It is that the training process has carved stable directions corresponding to relations the corpus makes systematic. These might be grammatical (present $\rightarrow$ past), morphological (noun $\rightarrow$ adjective), or social (profession $\rightarrow$ stereotype). The same machinery that learns the first also encodes the last.

We can estimate an average direction from many examples that embody a relation: for instance, from pairs like (\emph{Paris}, \emph{France}), (\emph{Berlin}, \emph{Germany}), and so on. Apply that direction to \emph{Rome} and we get something close to \emph{Italy}. Apply a direction learned from (\emph{he}, \emph{doctor}) and (\emph{he}, \emph{engineer}) to \emph{nurse} and we may drag the point toward \emph{she}. Whatever the archive consistently repeats, gradient descent will elevate into a vector field.

Saussure’s insistence that signs gain value from differences rather than intrinsic properties becomes literal here. There is no co-ordinate labelled ``royalty'' or ``gender''. There are only points and the differences between them. Directions that the loss function finds useful recur as transformations the model can perform again and again.

Once such a direction exists, it can be applied anywhere, whether or not we desire the effect. That is what makes this geometry powerful and dangerous: it is a library of learned moves, waiting to be re-used.

\section{Carving Continents: Training as Cartography}

The embedding space is not an abstract Platonic object. It is the cumulative by-product of a particular training pipeline operating on a particular collection of text over a particular stretch of time. It is an artefact.

We can think of the training process as a kind of negative cartography. Instead of surveying an existing continent, gradient descent scours a blank space with textual weather until continents condense out of the fog.

\subsection{Longue Dur\'{e}e: Pre-training}

At the deepest level lies pre-training. A transformer with randomly initialised parameters begins by guessing nonsense. It is shown sequences of tokens from a huge corpus spanning books, articles, code repositories, forum posts, transcripts. For each sequence $t_1,\dots,t_n$ it is asked to predict $t_{n+1}$. The discrepancy between its prediction and the true token defines a loss. Backpropagation computes gradients: partial derivatives of this loss with respect to millions, sometimes billions, of parameters. Each parameter is nudged in the direction that would have made the prediction slightly less wrong.

Repeat this trillions of times and the random matrices harden into a structure. The embedding vectors no longer sit scattered arbitrarily; they cluster around meaningful constellations. Attention heads specialise; some become experts in bracket matching, others in discourse structure, others in aligning responses with instructions. The manifold takes shape.

This stage is Braudel's \emph{longue dur\'{e}e} \cite{braudel1980}: slow-moving, infrastructural history. Decisions about which corpora to include or exclude, which languages to represent, which types of text to filter as ``low quality'', set the tectonic plates. Do patents and scientific papers dominate, or fan fiction and romance novels, or parliamentary debates and legislation? How much is drawn from social media? Are religious texts present, and in which translation? Each choice shifts mountain ranges.

Pre-training is expensive. It requires vast data centres, power, and legal cover for scraping and licensing. Only a handful of entities---large technology companies and state-backed labs---currently operate at this scale. The geometry we have just described is therefore not the neutral shape of ``language itself''. It is the frozen imprint of textual practices that powerful institutions have found it convenient or permissible to collect.

\subsection{Conjoncture: Fine-tuning}

Above the base geography lies a layer of more recent, medium-term formations: fine-tuning. Here, the already trained model is exposed to a narrower slice of text---say, medical records, legal contracts, corporate emails, source code---and the same next-token prediction process continues, but only on this specialised corpus.

Fine-tuning does not redraw the whole continent. It deepens certain valleys and raises certain ridges. A general-purpose basin for ``explaining things'' splits into sub-basins for clinical explanation, systems design, or appellate argument. The connotations of \emph{patient} move closer to \emph{consent form} and \emph{informed risk}. The point for \emph{indemnity} finds new neighbours.

This is Braudel's \emph{conjoncture} \cite{braudel1980}: cycles of profession, institution, and sector layered over the bedrock of civilisation. Fine-tuning is more affordable than pre-training; it can be carried out by hospitals, banks, law firms, or small labs with access to suitable data. Yet it builds on the same base. If the original model has a thin or distorted representation of some language or style, no amount of fine-tuning can conjure a continent where none exists.

\subsection{\'{E}v\'{e}nement: RLHF as Norm Shock}

A third depth operates not through more text but through judgement about text.

Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) proceeds in three stages. First, human annotators are presented with prompts and several candidate completions from the model. They rank these in order of preference, following guidelines that specify what counts as ``helpful'', ``harmful'', ``political'', ``harassing'', ``overly long'', and so on. Second, a reward model is trained to predict these rankings. Third, the base model is tuned with reinforcement learning to maximise the reward predicted by this auxiliary model.

This process adds a new gradient field to the manifold. Some directions now yield higher reward, others lower. In regions of the space corresponding to sensitive topics, the reward gradient can be very steep: any move toward certain frank statements about self-harm or hate speech will incur a penalty. Elsewhere the gradient is gentle: meandering around in harmless small talk does little harm.

Braudel's third register, the \emph{\'{e}v\'{e}nement}---surface events that jolt the system \cite{braudel1980}---has an analogue here. A chatbot scandal that triggers public outrage can lead to revised guidelines and a retrained reward model within weeks. A court ruling about what constitutes medical advice can reshape the gradient field around health-related language. None of this alters the underlying embedding directions, but it changes which trajectories are accessible without penalty.

Control over RLHF lies squarely with platform owners. They commission the guidelines, pay the annotators, design the reward models, and merge the resulting weights into deployment branches. Their sense of what speech is acceptable becomes a literal law of motion in the space. We should be clear: this is not censorship as post-processing; it is censorship as potential field.

\subsection{Surface: Adapters and Prompts}

The uppermost layers are the easiest to touch and the hardest to see.

One such layer consists of adapters: light modules, often low-rank additive matrices, inserted into the network. These allow the behaviour of a fixed base model to be adjusted with relatively little data and computational effort. A team might train an adapter so that the system can speak a particular dialect, imitate a house style, or restore a tone suppressed by RLHF. These deformations are local: they tilt trajectories around certain regions without reorienting the entire space.

Even thinner is the system prompt: a string of tokens inserted at the head of every context. It might read, in plain language, ``You are a helpful AI assistant. Answer concisely and avoid controversial topics.'' Those instructions are just more tokens. They are embedded into vectors like any others and become part of the input cloud that attention operates over.

Because the system prompt always appears first, it is especially salient to attention heads trained to look to the left for guidance. Because it is always paired with rewarded behaviour, it tends to acquire attractor status: many later queries pull their keys back toward the prompt's vectors.

Changing the system prompt does not touch any weight matrix. It requires no GPUs, only a configuration edit. Yet its effect can be dramatic. It is the closest thing the deployment stack has to a constitutional preamble.

\begin{formalbox}[title={Depths of Control in the Substrate}]
\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Layer}      & \textbf{Mechanism}                             & \textbf{Effect}                                  & \textbf{Typical Controllers} \\ \midrule
Pre-training        & Next-token loss on massive corpus              & Global manifold (\emph{longue dur\'{e}e})        & Big tech, state labs         \\
Fine-tuning         & Extra training on domain texts                 & Local basins (\emph{conjoncture})                & Corporations, institutions   \\
RLHF                & Reward model from human rankings               & Norm gradients (\emph{\'{e}v\'{e}nement})        & Platform owners              \\
Adapters            & Low-rank weight corrections                    & Local overlays                                   & Small labs, collectives      \\
