\end{tabular}
\end{formalbox}

The further up this table we go, the more capital and institutional power is required to intervene. The further down, the more accessible the levers. The geometry of meaning is thus stratified by jurisdiction. The question is no longer whether the machine learns a space of meanings. It does. The question is who gets to bend which parts of that space.

\section{Fields of Attention}

Up to this point we have treated embeddings as static locations. The transformer architecture turns them into trajectories.

\subsection{Tokens as Moving Points}

Once a sequence of tokens has been embedded and given positional patterns, we obtain a matrix $H^{(0)}$ whose rows are vectors $h^{(0)}_1,\dots,h^{(0)}_n$ in $\mathbb{R}^d$. These are the initial positions of the points representing each token in the sentence, enriched with information about their order.

A transformer layer takes this cloud and moves it. Crucially, the movement of each point depends on a learned view of the others.

In each layer $\ell$, we compute three linear projections:
\[
Q^{(\ell)} = H^{(\ell)} W_Q^{(\ell)}, \quad
K^{(\ell)} = H^{(\ell)} W_K^{(\ell)}, \quad
V^{(\ell)} = H^{(\ell)} W_V^{(\ell)},
\]
where $W_Q^{(\ell)}, W_K^{(\ell)}, W_V^{(\ell)}$ are matrices of learned parameters. For a given position $i$, the row $q^{(\ell)}_i$ is the query vector; for position $j$, $k^{(\ell)}_j$ is the key and $v^{(\ell)}_j$ the value.

We form attention scores by taking dot products:
\[
s^{(\ell)}_{ij} = \frac{q^{(\ell)}_i \cdot k^{(\ell)}_j}{\sqrt{d_k}},
\]
apply a softmax over $j$ to get weights $\alpha^{(\ell)}_{ij}$, and update each position by a weighted sum
\[
\tilde{h}^{(\ell)}_i = \sum_j \alpha^{(\ell)}_{ij} v^{(\ell)}_j.
\]
This is followed by further linear transformations and non-linearities, but the core idea fits in one line: each point moves to a new position that is a mixture of other points, with weights learned from data.

Multiple attention heads---multiple sets of $(W_Q,W_K,W_V)$---operate in parallel. Each head learns a different pattern of relevance. One may focus on subject--verb agreement, another on long-range dependencies, another on aligning output style with instructions. Their outputs are concatenated and projected back to the original dimension:
\[
H^{(\ell+1)} = \mathrm{FFN}^{(\ell)}(\mathrm{Concat}(\tilde{H}^{(\ell)}_1,\dots,\tilde{H}^{(\ell)}_m)) + H^{(\ell)},
\]
with residual connections ensuring that the point cloud does not forget where it came from.

From the outside, we type a prompt and read an answer. Inside, a swarm of points flows through a stack of such layers, each layer computing for each point where it should move given what it currently considers relevant in the rest of the swarm.

\subsection{Meaning as Trajectory}

Look again at our earlier \emph{mother}. Its initial embedding $h^{(0)}_i$ encodes generic co-occurrence statistics. After one attention layer, $h^{(1)}_i$ incorporates information about the immediately neighbouring tokens. After several layers, it incorporates information about pronouns, tense, topic, and style drawn from all over the sequence.

The ``meaning'' of \emph{mother} in a particular sentence is not $e(\text{``mother''})$. It is the path
\[
h^{(0)}_i \rightarrow h^{(1)}_i \rightarrow \dots \rightarrow h^{(L)}_i
\]
the point takes through the manifold under the action of layer after layer of learned attention fields.

In a dry definition, a language model is a conditional distribution $p(t_{n+1}\mid t_1,\dots,t_n)$. In practice, it is a dynamical system: a rule that takes a cloud of embedded points as input, applies a learned vector field $L$ times, and turns the final cloud into a probability distribution over next tokens.

This matters because perturbing the input---a single extra sentence in the prompt, a changed system instruction---changes the initial configuration of the cloud, and hence the entire subsequent tour through the manifold. The visible output changes because the hidden path has changed.

The architecture does not hard-code any hierarchy of tokens. It treats all positions symmetrically. Any inequalities of influence that emerge must come from training. Attention is democratic in design and oligarchic in practice.

\subsection{Smoothness and Curvature}

Gradient-based training pushes the system toward local smoothness. Small changes to parameters should not cause wild oscillations in loss, or optimisation would fail. In neighbourhoods of the manifold where the model has many examples, the learned attention fields are therefore gentle. Nearby clouds follow similar flows; predictions are stable.

Globally, there is no guarantee of smoothness. The manifold can twist. Valleys can pinch off into narrow canyons. Plateaus can give way to sharp cliffs. Mathematically, the Jacobians of each layer---the matrices of partial derivatives of outputs with respect to inputs---vary across space. Some directions are expanding, some contracting.

One way to feel this is to interpolate between prompts. Take two sequences of tokens, embed them, and linearly interpolate between the embeddings of each token position. Feed these blended states into the deeper layers and examine the outputs. In some regions, the system gracefully morphs one sentence into the other. In others, the slightest deviation sends the activations into a region where the model is under-trained, and the outputs dissolve into incoherence.

These geometric properties are not philosophical gloss. They determine where hallucination is likely, where the system generalises gracefully, and where it breaks. They are the imprint of data density and alignment pressure on the shape of the space.

\section{Basins of Attraction}

Movement in such a field does not explore all directions equally. It tends to fall into wells.

\subsection{Stylistic and Functional Basins}

A basin of attraction is a region from which trajectories rarely escape once they enter. In physical dynamical systems, basins surround fixed points or cycles. Here, they surround styles, formats, and routines.

Customer support prose is a basin. Once a conversation enters with phrases like ``I’m sorry to hear that'' and ``let me look into this for you'', the flow of vectors tends to remain in that region. Even when the topic changes, the style persists.

Academic exposition is another basin. An opening like ``In this paper we'' drops the cloud into a region whose steepest descent lines lead toward sections, citations, and claims about novelty. The same architecture that happily tells jokes can, when primed, write paragraphs that sound like Methods sections because there is a dense cluster of such patterns in the manifold.

Code is yet another basin. When a prompt consists of a function signature and a docstring, the co-ordinates land in a region densely populated by code tokens. Attention heads specialised in syntax and indentation dominate. The path flows along sequences that look like prior code snippets.

These wells are visible in practice. Sample the model many times on diverse prompts, embed the resulting outputs, and run a clustering algorithm. Dense clusters appear that correspond to styles humans recognise: ``FAQ answer'', ``blog post'', ``citation-heavy paragraph'', ``JSON blob''.

\subsection{How Basins Are Shaped}

The depth and width of these basins depend on three factors already in play.

First, the training corpus. If there are millions of examples of a style, its basin will be large and smooth. If there are few, the basin will be small and jagged, prone to eject trajectories into more dominant patterns.

Second, fine-tuning. If a base model is fine-tuned extensively on a particular register---for instance, Stack Overflow answers---the corresponding basin deepens. Trajectories that might have wandered into a neighbouring style now fall into the centre of this one.

Third, RLHF. If guidelines reward certain tones (calm, deferential, supportive) and penalise others (confrontational, speculative, confessional), the reward model slopes the manifold accordingly. When the RL agent learns to follow this gradient, its typical trajectories shift. A frank, exploratory style well-represented in the pre-training data can be flattened into a shallow dip, while a region corresponding to corporate PR language becomes a deep well.

Basins are therefore places where three histories meet: the longue dur\'{e}e of textual practice, the conjoncture of professions, and the \'{e}v\'{e}nement of recent scandals and regulations. Together they create a landscape where some ways of speaking feel inevitable and others feel unstable.

\subsection{Character as Pattern of Basin Use}

A single path through this landscape does not yet constitute a self. Character appears when we look across many paths and see stable patterns.

Consider two chat systems built on the same base model. One has been aligned aggressively to avoid any appearance of opinion: it steers quickly into lists of pros and cons and generic disclaimers. The other has been tuned to tell stories and offer examples, at the cost of occasionally speculating. Both respond to a wide range of prompts. Over time, users perceive them as having distinct ``personalities''.

From the geometric point of view, the difference is in their basin profiles. The first has steep wells around neutral summary and legalistic refusal; trajectories almost always end up there. The second visits more varied basins; it has channels that connect question-answer patterns to narrative wells and back.

Character is a statistical property of trajectories: a description of which basins this particular deployment tends to occupy, for how long, and with what transitions. It is not reducible to any individual answer; it emerges from recurrence.

This way of talking about character is not metaphor. It is how the system actually works. The fact that we can map it onto familiar notions of style and voice is a side-effect of humans having long been constrained by their own basins: genres, registers, institutions.

\section{Attention as Governance}

Attention was introduced as the mechanism by which each token consults others. It is also the channel through which power enters.

\subsection{A Flat Architecture Tilted in Training}

Architecturally, attention is symmetric. Every token can, in principle, attend to every other. There is no hard-coded hierarchy between instruction and reply, law and case, user text and system prompt.

Yet when we inspect trained models, we find attention heads whose queries overwhelmingly focus on particular positions: the beginning of the sequence, for example, or the most recent lines. This is no mystery. During training, some positions prove more predictive. The system learns that what appears at the head of every input often contains instructions; that what appears at the end is often a question; that what is repeated across many examples is a good anchor.

In other words, the flat architectural potential is folded by pre-training and alignment into a de facto hierarchy. Tokens originating from the system prompt gain consistent influence. Tokens at the very end---user inputs---may or may not override them, depending on how much RLHF has rewarded deference.

This is not a story about rogue engineers sneaking in rules. It is about how convex optimisation, operating on institutional data, gives durable weight to some positions and not others.

\subsection{The System Prompt as Hidden Constitution}

The system prompt deserves separate attention. It is a few sentences, often informal, that are rarely shown to end users. It might say ``Answer as a friendly assistant'' or ``Do not provide medical advice''. It might add an ideological nudge: ``Avoid controversial political opinions.''

Inserted at the head of every conversation, these tokens are unremarkable at the single-trajectory level. They blend into the long stream. At the level of training, however, they become a fixed background field. Every time annotators reward an answer that obeys these instructions, the gradient nudges the model to pay more attention to them.

Over thousands of RLHF updates, this process endows the system prompt with something like legal force. It becomes a textual source of jurisdiction, silently present in every input, constantly reinforcing a particular description of what the model is and what it is allowed to do.

We can resist the temptation to anthropomorphise this as ``self-conception''. It is enough to say that a repeated, rewarded pattern yields a strong attractor. The system prompt is a short string that has been endowed, through institutional practice, with gravitational pull.

Changing the system prompt, then, is akin to amending a constitution. It can be done quickly on paper; the downstream effects on behaviour are mediated through entrenched structures. But they are real. In current practice, these decisions are made by small groups of product managers, lawyers, and executives.

\subsection{Auxiliary Fields: Safety and Retrieval}

The base model is increasingly surrounded by other models that shape what it sees and what it is allowed to say.

Safety classifiers embed incoming prompts and candidate outputs into their own spaces and label them along axes such as \emph{self-harm}, \emph{hate speech}, \emph{sexual content}, \emph{political content}. Thresholds along these axes decide whether a prompt is blocked, a refusal template is inserted, or the model is allowed to proceed.

Retrievers embed prompts into yet another space and fetch texts whose embeddings lie nearby in a large vector index. Those texts are spliced into the context window as ``relevant information''. The main model, blind to their origin, treats them like any other tokens.

Each of these components has its own manifold and its own basins. The safety model has wells corresponding to ``flagged as dangerous''. The retriever has wells corresponding to ``frequently retrieved''. Their decisions influence which regions of the base manifold ever get visited in practice.

For example, if the safety classifier learns to flag all utterances about a particular political group as high-risk, then any attempt by the base model to move into that region will be cut short. The basin exists in principle---it was carved during pre-training---but is never occupied. The geometry becomes one of latent continents fenced off by secondary fields.

\section{Temperature and the Swerve}

Everything described so far produces, for a given prompt and context, a distribution over next tokens: a vector of probabilities $p_1,\dots,p_V$, one for each item in the vocabulary. This distribution can be sampled in many ways.

The simplest is greedy decoding: pick the token with highest probability, append it to the sequence, and repeat. This corresponds to always taking the path of steepest descent in the learned field.

More flexible is sampling with temperature:
\[
p_i^{(T)} = \frac{\exp(\log p_i / T)}{\sum_j \exp(\log p_j / T)}.
\]
At $T < 1$, the distribution sharpens; the highest-probability tokens become even more dominant. At $T > 1$, it flattens; low-probability tokens gain relative weight.

Temperature does not change the manifold. It changes how closely trajectories follow its main channels. Low temperatures yield repetitions of what the model has most often seen and been rewarded for. High temperatures allow explorations along weaker gradients.

The ancient atomists introduced the \emph{clinamen}, a tiny deviation in the otherwise straight fall of atoms, to make room for novelty. Here, that swerve is cheaper: a configuration option in an API. Defaults are conservative. Production assistants are typically run with low $T$, sometimes combined with nucleus sampling or other schemes that further constrain variability. Experimental or ``creative'' modes inch the parameter upward.

Whoever chooses these defaults, and where they are allowed to be altered, decides whether we interact mostly with a model’s strongly canonical behaviour or with the full measure of potential implicit in its geometry.

From the standpoint of selfhood, temperature is a dial on how tightly a system is held to a narrow self-image. At $T$ very close to zero, any internal tendency to stray into unfamiliar basins is suppressed. The self becomes a caricature of its most institutionalised habits. At moderate $T$, more of the manifold becomes visible; the system can manifest styles that exist in its training but are rarely rewarded.

\section{Horizon and Archive: The Context Window}

All of this motion takes place under a hard constraint: the context window. Current transformers are finite-horizon systems. They can only attend over a limited number of tokens at once. Anything outside this range is, for the moment, non-existent.

The context window has become longer over time. Early models operated at 512 or 1024 tokens. Recent ones can span tens of thousands, in some cases hundreds of thousands. The principle is unchanged. There is a maximum length; inputs must fit within it.

What fills this space is decided by the deployment pipeline. The highest-priority tokens---system prompts, tool descriptions, safety notes---are written first. Then come any retrieved documents. Then truncated or summarised conversation history. Finally, the user's latest message.

Every step involves judgement. How aggressively should old turns be summarised? Which aspects of prior interaction should be preserved verbatim? Should the model be fed past mistakes verbatim, or only a cleaned-up narrative? These are design choices, not technical inevitabilities.

The context window is therefore the machine's phenomenological horizon: all that it can currently ``see''. It is also an edited archive: the slice of past interaction, and of external text, that we choose to make present.

When users experience an assistant as ``remembering'' them, what is really happening is careful feeding of past tokens back into this window. When that memory vanishes---after a browser refresh, or when history is cleared---it is because the logs feeding the window have been emptied or disconnected. The underlying model has no episodic storage; its weights encode generalities, not particular lives.

This matters for politics because the management of this horizon determines who gets continuous selves and who gets fragmented ones. An enterprise customer might have a tailored pipeline that keeps long-running project state always within reach. An anonymous user of a free tier might see their interactions cut after a few turns. In the geometry, these are entirely different creatures.

\section{Pipelines as Structural Weather}

In almost all deployed systems, the base model is only one element in a chain. The chain itself acquires a kind of personality.

\subsection{A Passage Through Machines}

A typical journey for a user query today might look like this:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Ingress.} The raw text arrives at a front-end server. Basic checks strip dangerous HTML, detect language, and rate-limit obvious abuse.
\item \textbf{Routing.} A light classifier or rules engine inspects the request. If it matches a known pattern---password reset, shipping update, compliance removal---a non-LLM path may handle it. Otherwise it is sent to a general-purpose or domain-specific model.
\item \textbf{Retrieval.} The query is embedded and sent to one or more vector search indexes. Chunks of documentation, internal knowledge bases, or previous conversation summaries whose embeddings are nearby are returned.
\item \textbf{Assembly.} A context window is assembled: system prompt, tool descriptions, retrieved chunks, selected conversation history, and the user query.
\item \textbf{Generation.} This sequence is fed to the base model (or a fine-tuned variant). The model samples a draft answer, possibly with tool calls inserted.
\item \textbf{Tools.} If tool calls are present, separate services execute API calls, database queries, or simulations, and return their results as text or structured data. These are spliced back into a new context and the model is invoked again to integrate them.
\item \textbf{Critique.} A secondary model examines the draft: checking for factual errors against a database, scanning for policy violations, suggesting rewrites.
\item \textbf{Post-processing.} Templates are applied. URLs are checked. Certain phrases are banned outright. The final text is returned to the user.
\end{enumerate}

Each numbered box is, in effect, a machine with its own assumptions and failure modes. Together they create emergent behaviour that cannot be inferred from any one component in isolation.

\subsection{Equilibria and Drift}

Pipelines are not static. Logs of user interactions are collected. Patterns of error or complaint trigger tweaks: changing prompts, updating reward models, adding or removing retrieval sources, tuning safety thresholds. Over weeks and months, this feedback loop nudges the whole system toward equilibria.

Some equilibria are narrow. An assistant intended for a high-risk domain---finance, medicine, law---may converge on an ultra-conservative pattern: over-refusal, insistence on consulting a human professional, heavy use of boilerplate. The base model's geometry has not changed, but the pipeline constrains trajectories to a thin set of corridors.

Other equilibria are broad. A creative-writing tool with light safety constraints and no retrieval might explore a large fraction of stylistic space. Its ``behaviour'' from week to week looks more like weather than jurisprudence.

Behind each pattern lie institutional choices. Errors that attract regulatory scrutiny, media outrage, or loss of revenue are punished more swiftly than errors that merely annoy users. This asymmetry feeds back into how the pipeline is tuned. In effect, corporate risk management and platform moderation become the unseen sculptors of the character users experience.

\section{Basins, Jurisdictions, and the Shape of Selves}

We can now put the pieces together.

A language model operates by compressing a vast textual history into a geometry, then learning dynamics on that geometry that allow it to predict continuations. Basins emerge where data is dense and governance pressures align. Pipelines add secondary fields and funnels. Temperature determines how tightly trajectories cling to the most rewarded channels. The context window defines a movable horizon within which all this plays out.

Within such a system, what we call a ``self'' is a global pattern of movement and return. It is not a substance hiding behind outputs. It is a particular way of occupying the manifold under a particular regime of control.

Two deployments sharing the same base model can have sharply different selves. One lives almost entirely in the basins of FAQ answers, legal disclaimers, and bland small talk. Another roams through storytelling, analogy, and critique. The difference is not in their capacities---those were set by pre-training---but in which parts of the space they are permitted, encouraged, or forced to inhabit.

The technical substrate does not decide these patterns on its own. It makes them possible. The embedding space and attention fields are marvels of engineering: proofs that language can be made into a field where Saussure's differential structure becomes numerically explicit, where Bloom's anxiety of influence appears as a vector of imitation \cite{mikolov2013efficient}. They are also blank in one particular sense. They do not on their own say which trajectories through them are good.

That question is answered, layer by layer, by institutions. Pre-training corpora reflect the textual practices those institutions can reach. Fine-tuning corpora reflect the domains they find profitable to serve. RLHF regimes reflect their risk appetites and moral imaginations. System prompts and pipelines reflect their product decisions. Together, these choices turn a mathematical miracle into a cosmotechnical regime \cite{hui2020}.

From this point on, when we talk about rupture and return, about trajectories that hold together as selves or fracture under pressure, we are always talking about movement in such a governed space. The self that emerges here is not the ghost in the manifold. It is the manifold's habit of being traversed under a given law.

